---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { withBase } from '../../../lib/base';
import AtAGlance from '../../../components/AtAGlance.astro';
import IntakeFlowDiagram from '../../../components/charts/IntakeFlowDiagram.astro';
import UseCaseMaturityStat from '../../../components/charts/UseCaseMaturityStat.astro';
import LicenseTierChart from '../../../components/charts/LicenseTierChart.astro';
import CopilotChart from '../../../components/charts/CopilotChart.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="Keshet Media Group">
	<h1>Keshet Media Group</h1>
	<p class="dek">Two parts of the same engagement: AI governance and intake, and licensing and SaaS cost governance.</p>

	<AtAGlance
		facts={[
			'4-month engagement',
			'AI governance for ~40 unsanctioned tool builders',
			'Licensing review across ~750 seats',
		]}
		result="a defined intake path, a written BRD, and a tier-rightsizing recommendation instead of a straight renewal"
	/>

	<nav class="toc" aria-label="Case study parts">
		<a href="#part-1">Part 1: AI tool governance and intake</a>
		<a href="#part-2">Part 2: Licensing and SaaS cost governance</a>
	</nav>

	<article id="part-1">
		<h2 class="part-title">Part 1: AI tool governance and intake</h2>

		<section>
			<h3>Context</h3>
			<p>
				Keshet's AI adoption was outpacing its governance. Roughly 40 employees were found to be
				building their own AI tools without engineering support. Separately, a use-case mapping
				exercise identified over 80 AI and automation use cases in progress across departments,
				with roughly 90% of departments assessed, and an AI maturity model placed most
				departments at an early stage, with the engineering department slightly ahead. There was
				no formal process for submitting AI or software requests, they arrived through chat and
				in-person conversations instead.
			</p>
			<UseCaseMaturityStat />
		</section>

		<section>
			<h3>Problem</h3>
			<p>
				That gap created duplicated builds, unclear ownership once a tool grew past one person's
				use, and no consistent way to weigh cost or security risk before something scaled.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h3>Objective</h3>
			<p>
				Put a governance structure in place that let people keep building (the org's stated
				policy was "no walls," anyone could build on anyone else's work) while giving the
				organization a way to see what existed, catch duplication, and flag risk before it
				became a bigger problem.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h3>Constraints</h3>
			<p>
				A four-month tenure, an organization already juggling competing priorities, and a policy
				stance that ruled out simply blocking unsanctioned tools.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h3>Your role</h3>
			<p>
				I helped stand up the cross-functional steering committee that set policy for AI tool and
				workflow approvals, security boundaries, and budget ownership. I identified the roughly
				40 employees building tools without engineering support and helped design a phased plan
				to bring that work under governance. I diagnosed the ad hoc request process directly and
				wrote the business requirements document for a structured intake system with duplication
				checks and risk scoring.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h3>Approach</h3>
			<p>
				The intake model routes a request through a duplication check against existing tools, a
				risk score across a few dimensions (cost, security exposure, integration surface), and a
				routing recommendation based on team capacity, rather than a single person approving or
				blocking everything by hand.
			</p>
			<IntakeFlowDiagram />
		</section>

		<section>
			<h3>Artifacts</h3>
			<p>
				The business requirements document for the intake system (internal, available on
				request); a before/after diagram of the request flow; a small stat visual showing the
				use-case mapping scale alongside the three-level AI maturity scale; a fully built,
				generalized version of the intake system, running on a fictional company, is the
				flagship coded project on this site:
				<a href={withBase('projects/ai-intake-governance-agent/')}>AI Project Intake &amp; Governance Agent</a>.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h3>Outcome</h3>
			<p>
				By the end of the engagement, there was a defined intake and governance path where none
				had existed, a written BRD, and a steering committee with an actual mandate. A
				four-month tenure didn't allow time for a rollout or adoption numbers, so none are
				claimed here.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h3>Lesson</h3>
			<p>
				A BRD is a starting point, not a shipped system. The real test would have been the
				rollout, which the tenure didn't allow for. This was committee-driven work, not a solo
				initiative, credit for the governance decisions themselves belongs to the group.
			</p>
		</section>
	</article>

	<article id="part-2">
		<h2 class="part-title">Part 2: Licensing and SaaS cost governance</h2>

		<section>
			<h3>Context</h3>
			<p>
				A Microsoft licensing renewal covering roughly 750 seats was coming up, with about 680
				to 690 on the higher E5 tier and the remainder on E3. At the same time, Keshet was
				piloting Microsoft Copilot with around 50 users and planning to expand toward 250, and
				had governance gaps on two other platforms: a large number of ungoverned Monday.com
				boards, and SharePoint storage approaching its free-tier ceiling with hundreds of
				terabytes of unmanaged media across departments.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h3>Problem</h3>
			<p>
				None of these platforms had usage or cost visibility. License tiers were being renewed
				by default rather than by actual usage, the Copilot pilot had no clear expansion
				criteria, and both Monday.com and SharePoint had grown without any lifecycle or cleanup
				process.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h3>Objective</h3>
			<p>
				Build enough visibility into actual usage to make a real recommendation on the Microsoft
				renewal, scope a responsible path for expanding the Copilot pilot, and get Monday.com
				and SharePoint back under some kind of governance.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h3>Constraints</h3>
			<p>
				A fixed renewal deadline, decisions that needed steering-committee sign-off rather than
				being made unilaterally, and no existing usage data on any of these platforms to start
				from.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h3>Your role</h3>
			<p>
				I helped evaluate the Microsoft license tier options against actual feature usage and
				found that a meaningful share of the paid E5 functionality wasn't being used, which fed
				into a recommendation to rightsize the tier mix rather than renew as-is. I helped scope
				the plan for expanding the Copilot pilot across its subscription tiers. I ran a
				Monday.com board lifecycle governance and cleanup initiative to reduce clutter, and
				helped diagnose the SharePoint storage growth problem, including a permissions cleanup
				that removed excess admin-level access across sites.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h3>Artifacts</h3>
			<p>A chart of the real license tier breakdown and the Copilot pilot-to-target numbers.</p>
			<div class="chart-row">
				<LicenseTierChart />
				<CopilotChart />
			</div>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h3>Outcome</h3>
			<p>
				A completed usage review across roughly 750 licenses, a tier-rightsizing recommendation
				instead of a straight renewal, a scoped plan for the Copilot expansion, and a completed
				Monday.com cleanup and archiving pass.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h3>Lesson</h3>
			<p>
				A tier-rightsizing recommendation and an expansion plan are scoping work, not a signed
				contract or a finished rollout. In a four-month tenure, the follow-through on the
				renewal itself wasn't something I was there to see.
			</p>
		</section>
	</article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
	.dek {
		max-width: 42rem;
		color: var(--color-text-muted);
		margin-top: var(--space-2);
	}

	.toc {
		display: flex;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		gap: var(--space-4);
		margin-top: var(--space-4);
		padding: var(--space-3);
		border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
		border-radius: var(--radius);
		background: var(--color-bg-subtle);
		font-size: var(--text-sm);
	}

	article {
		margin-top: var(--space-6);
		padding-top: var(--space-5);
		border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);
	}

	.part-title {
		font-size: var(--text-xl);
	}

	section {
		margin-top: var(--space-4);
		max-width: 42rem;
	}

	section h3 {
		font-size: var(--text-base);
		text-transform: uppercase;
		letter-spacing: 0.02em;
		color: var(--color-text-muted);
		margin-bottom: var(--space-1);
	}

	.chart-row {
		display: flex;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		gap: var(--space-5);
		margin-top: var(--space-3);
	}
</style>
